<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Abbey of Kempten</h1><p class="st-meta">1213 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Kempten</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Benedictine abbey in the Allgäu whose prince-abbots ruled a sizeable territory and rebuilt Kempten in baroque splendour after the Thirty Years War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Benedictine abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a> was founded in the mid 8th century near the old Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodunum">Cambodunum</a> and enjoyed the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne's</a> wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildegard-of-the-vinzgau">Hildegard</a>. It gathered estates throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a>, and from 1213 its abbots were recognised as princes of the Empire, ruling a secular territory.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince-abbots, drawn from the nobility, governed farmers and small towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a> through officials and a strong territorial administration. They clashed constantly with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-kempten">Free Imperial City of Kempten</a>, which bought its freedom from the abbey in 1525, the year their own peasants rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">Peasants' War</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years War Abbot Roman Giel von Gielsberg built the Residenz and the Basilica of St. Lorenz.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The abbey was secularised in 1803 and annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, which merged the abbey town with the Protestant imperial city into a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a>. The baroque Residenz and basilica survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-abbey-of-kempten.jpg" alt="Prince-Abbey of Kempten" width="320" height="401"><figcaption>Image: Lubiesque · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kempten,_Imperial_City_and_Imperial_Abbey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1213 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kempten</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Roman Giel von Gielsberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürststift Kempten</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1508&amp;focus=prince-abbey-of-kempten" aria-label="Prince-Abbey of Kempten on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4921 505 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Abbey of Kempten lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5241 761L5229 694M5241 761L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5241 761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5241 761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1508&amp;focus=prince-abbey-of-kempten">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
